load @term/seed/code/structure/manifold
  find manifold

# Horocycles and horospheres, the surfaces centered at infinity, the bridge from the curved bulk to a flat lattice.

# A horocycle: in the plane, the limit of circles through a fixed point as their centers run off to an ideal point, a
# curve perpendicular to all the geodesics aiming at that ideal point. Equivalently a circle of infinite radius. In the
# Poincare disk it is a circle internally tangent to the boundary; in the upper half-space a horizontal line. The form
# carries the manifold.
form horocycle
  link manifold, like manifold

# A horosphere: the higher-dimensional horocycle, the limit of growing spheres tangent to one ideal point. Its crucial
# property is that it is INTRINSICALLY FLAT: a being living on a horosphere measures ordinary Euclidean geometry, even
# though the horosphere sits curved inside hyperbolic space. So a Euclidean lattice can live on a horosphere. The
# substrate's flat-lattice model is exactly this: the curvature is in the embedding, not in the tiling on the surface.
# The form carries the manifold the horosphere sits in.
form horosphere
  link manifold, like manifold

# Horospherical coordinates: in the upper half-space model the horospheres are the horizontal planes, and the geodesics
# aimed at the top ideal point are the vertical lines, giving a clean coordinate system (a Euclidean plane stacked along
# an exponential height). This is the coordinate frame in which the flat-lattice picture of the substrate is read. The
# form carries the manifold.
form horospherical-coordinates
  link manifold, like manifold
